Contrasts
Derivations for the planning formulas of Section 2.4.3, on which Section 2.3 draws for \(W = 1\).
Derivation of Equation 11. Decomposing the test statistic around the truth gives \[
\begin{aligned}
Z &= \frac{\hat{C} - C_0}{\sqrt{\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}]}} = \frac{\hat{C} - C}{\sqrt{\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}]}} + \frac{\delta}{\sqrt{\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}]}} \\[4pt]
&= \frac{\hat{C} - C}{\sqrt{\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}]}} + \lambda\,\sqrt{\frac{\bar{V}_C}{N\,\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}]}},
\end{aligned}
\] where the first term is asymptotically standard normal and the factor multiplying \(\lambda\) tends to one in probability, since \(N\,\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}] \xrightarrow{P} \bar{V}_C\). The approximation is rigorous under local alternatives \(\delta = \delta^\ast/\sqrt{N}\), along which the two conditions of Section 2.1 continue to hold; for fixed \(\delta\) the estimation error of the variance is multiplied by \(\lambda\), so Equation 11 is accurate whenever \(\lambda\) is small relative to \(\sqrt{N}\).
Derivation of Equation 12 and Equation 13. Under \(Z \overset{a}{\sim} N(\lambda, 1)\) the two-sided test rejects when \(|Z| &gt; z_{1-\alpha/2}\), so \[
\begin{aligned}
\pi_{two} &= P[Z &gt; z_{1-\alpha/2}] + P[Z &lt; -z_{1-\alpha/2}] \\[4pt]
&= \Phi_{0,1}(\lambda - z_{1-\alpha/2}) + \Phi_{0,1}(-\lambda - z_{1-\alpha/2});
\end{aligned}
\] at \(\delta = 0\) the sum equals \(\alpha\), confirming the size of the test. The second term is the probability of rejecting with the wrong sign; it is negligible unless \(\lambda\) is near zero, and omitting it understates power, so the one-term form of Equation 12 is conservative. The one-sided tests reject on one side only, \(\pi_{right} = P[Z &gt; z_{1-\alpha}] = \Phi_{0,1}(\lambda - z_{1-\alpha})\) and \(\pi_{left} = P[Z &lt; -z_{1-\alpha}] = \Phi_{0,1}(-\lambda - z_{1-\alpha})\), without a wrong-sign term.
Derivation of Equation 14 and Equation 15. Decomposing the two one-sided statistics around the truth as above gives \[
\begin{aligned}
Z_L &= \frac{\hat{C} - C_L}{\sqrt{\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}]}} = \frac{\hat{C} - C}{\sqrt{\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}]}} + \lambda_L\,\sqrt{\frac{\bar{V}_C}{N\,\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}]}}, \\[4pt]
Z_U &= \frac{C_U - \hat{C}}{\sqrt{\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}]}} = -\frac{\hat{C} - C}{\sqrt{\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}]}} + \lambda_U\,\sqrt{\frac{\bar{V}_C}{N\,\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}]}},
\end{aligned}
\] so \(Z_L \overset{a}{\sim} N(\lambda_L, 1)\) and \(Z_U \overset{a}{\sim} N(\lambda_U, 1)\), tied together by \(Z_L + Z_U = (C_U - C_L)/\sqrt{\widehat{\text{Var}}[\hat{C}]} \approx w\), so that \(Z_U \approx w - Z_L\) and \(\lambda_L + \lambda_U = w\). The interval null is rejected when \(Z_L &lt; -z_{1-\alpha}\) or \(Z_U &lt; -z_{1-\alpha}\), the latter being \(Z_L &gt; w + z_{1-\alpha}\); the two events are disjoint, and with \(\lambda_L - w = -\lambda_U\), \[
\begin{aligned}
\pi_{int} &= P[Z_L &lt; -z_{1-\alpha}] + P[Z_L &gt; w + z_{1-\alpha}] \\[4pt]
&= \Phi_{0,1}(-\lambda_L - z_{1-\alpha}) + \Phi_{0,1}(-\lambda_U - z_{1-\alpha}).
\end{aligned}
\] Equivalence is declared when \(Z_L &gt; z_{1-\alpha}\) and \(Z_U &gt; z_{1-\alpha}\), that is, \(z_{1-\alpha} &lt; Z_L &lt; w - z_{1-\alpha}\), an interval that is empty unless \(w &gt; 2 z_{1-\alpha}\); hence, with \(w - \lambda_L = \lambda_U\), \[
\begin{aligned}
\pi_{equiv} &= P[z_{1-\alpha} &lt; Z_L &lt; w - z_{1-\alpha}] \\[4pt]
&= \Phi_{0,1}(w - z_{1-\alpha} - \lambda_L) - \Phi_{0,1}(z_{1-\alpha} - \lambda_L) \\[4pt]
&= \Phi_{0,1}(\lambda_L - z_{1-\alpha}) + \Phi_{0,1}(\lambda_U - z_{1-\alpha}) - 1.
\end{aligned}
\]
Derivation of Equation 16 and Equation 17. Setting \(\Phi_{0,1}(|\lambda| - z_{1-\alpha/2}) = 1 - \beta\) in Equation 12 gives \(|\lambda| = z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta}\), that is, \(|\delta|\sqrt{N/\bar{V}_C} = z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta}\); solving for \(N\) and for \(|\delta|\) yields Equation 16, and the one-sided case replaces \(z_{1-\alpha/2}\) by \(z_{1-\alpha}\). For the minimum effect test with a true value beyond the margin by \(|\delta| - \delta_0\), the nearer-margin term of Equation 14 dominates and is the one-sided power with noncentrality \((|\delta| - \delta_0)\sqrt{N/\bar{V}_C}\), which gives \(N_{int}\). For equivalence at \(\delta = 0\), \(\lambda_L = \lambda_U = \delta_0\sqrt{N/\bar{V}_C}\) and Equation 15 becomes \(2\,\Phi_{0,1}(\delta_0\sqrt{N/\bar{V}_C} - z_{1-\alpha}) - 1 = 1 - \beta\), that is, \(\Phi_{0,1}(\delta_0\sqrt{N/\bar{V}_C} - z_{1-\alpha}) = 1 - \beta/2\), which gives \(N_{equiv}\). For \(\delta \neq 0\) the power at fixed \(N\) is largest at \(\delta = 0\), since Equation 15 with \(\lambda_L + \lambda_U = w\) fixed is maximized at \(\lambda_L = \lambda_U\), so the root of \(\pi_{equiv} = 1 - \beta\) exceeds the closed form.
